<article class="env-remark" id="00KR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00KR">Remark <span data-tag="00KR">2.4.3.4</span> <span class="named">(Relationship with Ordinary Path Categories)</span>.</a> Let $Q$ be a partially ordered set and let $\mathrm{Gr}(Q)$ denote the associated directed graph, given concretely by </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Vert}( \mathrm{Gr}(Q) ) = Q \quad \quad \operatorname{Edge}( \mathrm{Gr}(Q) ) = \{  (x,y) \in Q: x &lt; y \} . \]
</div>
<p> Then the path category $\operatorname{Path}[ \mathrm{Gr}(Q) ]$ of Construction <a data-tag="00HK" href="/tag/00HK">1.3.7.1</a> is the underlying category of the simplicial category $\operatorname{Path}[Q]_{\bullet }$ of Notation <a data-tag="00KN" href="/tag/00KN">2.4.3.1</a> (see Remark <a data-tag="00JL" href="/tag/00JL">2.3.5.2</a>). In other words, we can regard $\operatorname{Path}[Q]_{\bullet }$ as a simplicially enriched version of $\operatorname{Path}[ \mathrm{Gr}(Q) ]$. Beware that the simplicial enrichment is nontrivial in general: that is, the simplicial mapping sets $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{Path}[Q]}( x, y)_{\bullet }$ are usually not constant. </p>
</article>
